ing to be inducements and one of them  and it's an open secret is that if you  give enough money to a political party  you may find yourself able to be  suggested as being a potential peer it's  not a great system at all i i don't like  it anymore more than anyone else uh  but as i say  what are the other options  james it hasn't been a good week for the  government has it can you give us the  latest update with owen patterson  jeffrey cox and the cash for peerages  story yeah the government has basically  created a scandal that has run all week  and i think is beginning to do real  damage to the tory party  it's actions in trying to stay the um  the guilty verdict against her and  paterson  and then gonna try and reform the rules  on the back of that was so brazen it has  created a backlash  it has got everyone looking at  mp's second jobs and it has put boris  johnson in a very difficult position  he's having to choose whether to be on  the right side of public opinion uh or  the right side of his own mps because i  think if you're judging from what mps  say they're getting in their inboxes  there's no doubt that most of the public  question why mps need to have  the ability to earn money other than  their 82 000 pounds a year uh  parliamentary salary  yet uh at the same time  mps tory mps and particularly more more  traditional tory mps think uh a  they have these outside interests which  they are reluctant to give up and they  also think this is not an invalid point  that if you basically bar mps from doing  any other work other than their  parliamentary work you are going to  limit parliament to those who wish to be  professional politicians  and you are going to end you know the  ranks of kind of qcmps that you had you  know i think people think people like  ken clark or michael howard  and  i think that would narrow the experience  available to parliament and i think it  would denude parliament of quality i  think one of the problems we've already  got at the moment  is that there is a tendency for uh and  theresa may is an honorable exception to  this there's a tendency for former prime  ministers to kind of quit the comments  as soon as they've left office i think  if you if you if you banned all outside  interests i think you would find that  nearly all former cabinet ministers quit  the commons as soon as they left office  douglas you wonder in your column this  week whether trying to reform the lords  and the commas misses a crucial point do  our representatives even have that much  power in parliament anymore  yes this is something which i've been  reflecting on for a long time as i  mentioned not just in relation to the  house of lords but the house of commons  as well um you know remember one of the  the main uh the main slogan of the  brexiteers was take back control  and i say in my column this week uh  when the british public voted to take  back control from unelected bureaucrats  in brussels  i do not think that we intended to take  back that control and give it to  unelected bureaucrats in westminster  and this is a subject which as james  well knows has been bubbling for a long  time among both mps and peers about who  actually has primacy in their own houses  um now it seems to me without going into  the details of it it seems to me the  owen patterson affair has an awful lot  um  that it suggests about our democracy not  functioning well uh i believe that the  oversight that occurred was seriously  lacking  uh and it and you know  basically what occurred would have been  impossible in a judicial set up owen  patterson effectively wanted his day in  court was not given it partly because  the person who was judging him is not a  judge  um she's an absolutely typical  archetypal bureaucrat  um  and there was so much wrong in her  investigation but then you end up in  this position of course which was made  much worse by the the ridiculous  u-turning of the boris johnson  government i mean if you're going to  spend political capital as they were  going to do  spend it and and and see it through  don't spend it and then then you turn at  the first uh cries of complaint but but  the real thing is not just the oversight  of mps and the oversight of mp's um  extracurricular or extra activities  but also in the same in the house of  lords  james douglas is pointing out here that  he thinks that the oversight mechanisms  currently in place can sometimes be too  powerful that's certainly something the  allies of owen patterson have been  saying these past few weeks on the flip  side of course especially in light of  the jeffrey cox revelations you have  people saying that there simply hasn't  been enough oversight but it feels to me  like we're getting to a far broader  point here and perhaps a more crucial  one which is  what is the job of an mp and how much  time and dedication should be put into  that craft as opposed to other kinds of  work  so i i personally think that having mps  who  uh carry on with their former  professions and trades while they are  back bench mps  can be healthy um for example just  constitutionally we have an attorney  general and a solicitor general in our  system and an advocate general for  scotland they all need to be lawyers so  you do need a certain number of lawyers  to be practicing in the house of commons  and you know i think you know michael  howard and ken clark are examples of the  fact that you can combine a successful  legal career at the bar with a  successful parliamentary and political  career  and  that i think is healthy i think where  things become more dubious  is when mps are being paid for kind of  political intelligence or things like  that  rather than thought for carrying on with  the jobs that they were doing but i also  think we have to make choices as a  country uh i think we lament the fact  that there are so many professional  politicians in the house of commons we  lament the fact that there are so many  if one was